(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).lsat=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.lsat.full||[]).push({
  num: 2, part: `Part One: The Argument`, title: `What They Had to Believe`, weeks: `November to December 2026`,
  tasks: [`LR01`,`LR02`,`LR04`,`LR05`,`LR06`,`RC01`,`RC05`,`RC06`,`RC07`],
  cast: [
    [`Ana Brennan`,`Appellate lawyer on Federal Boulevard, 58. Lost Luis's direct appeal in 2010. Took the case for nothing.`],
    [`Luis Ruiz`,`Dani's father, 61, at Sterling. Calls Wednesdays at seven. Has never met his granddaughter.`],
    [`Teo Ruiz`,`Dani's brother, 29. Fixes things instead of visiting.`],
    [`Bianca Medina`,`Teo's partner, 28. Lupe's mother.`],
    [`Lupe Ruiz`,`Teo and Bianca's daughter, 6. Draws on everything.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Thanksgiving. Thursday, November 26. Dani did the turkey her mother's way, in at six, oven door open every twenty minutes so she could tell it what she thought of it. Teo's furnace ran all day without a click. Sage, and that burnt dust smell off the registers you only get the first real cold night. Every year she forgot it. Every year it got her in the hall.`,
    `They came at two. Bianca had a casserole she said not to judge. Teo stood in the kitchen doorway with a store pie, both hands on it like it was evidence, and Dani had to pry it off him. Lupe's crayons rattled around in a pencil box whose latch had quit in September. She was six. Anything flat was a canvas.`,
    `One picture of their father lived on the fridge, under a pizza magnet. Her fourteenth birthday, 2006, this table. Him home from the day shift in the red work jacket, laughing at whoever had the camera. In front of him on the table, a paper party hat, still flat. Lupe had never met him. She climbed on a chair for a better look and stayed up there a long time.`,
    `She drew right through dinner (holiday rules, Bianca said). Halfway through the potatoes she held it up. <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"This is Grandpa."</span> A man in red. Baseball cap. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Grandpa doesn't wear hats, mija."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"Why not?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"You can ask him someday."</span> Lupe chewed on that. Then she drew a purple horse underneath him, so he'd have something to ride, and put the cap on the horse.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"I made his jacket red,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Lupe">"Like the picture. Is that his favorite jacket? Tía. Is the red jacket his..."</span> Teo's fork went down. Carefully, like it might go off. Lights, he said. He'd left his lights on. Out the back door, and he didn't come back in for twenty minutes. His truck was at the curb. From the window over the sink Dani could see its lights were off.`,
    `Bianca kept her eyes on the back door. <span class="said" data-who="Bianca">"He's always slept bad,"</span> she said, low, mostly to her plate. <span class="said" data-who="Bianca">"Since Halloween it's every night. Wakes up at three, goes out and sits in the truck. Won't tell me."</span> She looked over like Dani might know. Dani didn't. Said so. Bianca nodded the way you nod at a forecast.`,
    `Could Grandpa have the drawing? Lupe asked it twice. Dani said yes before she'd thought about it.`,
    `When Teo came back in he didn't sit down. He ran the water and did the dishes, all of them, even the roasting pan she'd meant to soak till Saturday. She dried. She told him about the drawing, that Lupe wanted Grandpa to have it. He scrubbed the same corner of the pan a long time.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Put her name on it,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Teo">"Not mine."</span>`,
    `Friday morning it went in an envelope with the long address, the one with his number in it, and LUPE on the back in Dani's printing, into the blue box on the corner. First thing from Teo's side of the family he'd get in ten years.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ana's Pencil: what they had to believe`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Under the line`,
        body: [`The radiator's knocking, which means the laundromat turned the boiler up, which means it's below freezing on Federal. Dicta's taken the warm end of the rug. Last time I made you find where an argument goes. Tonight we look at the road between where it starts and where it ends, because that's where arguments cheat.`,
          `Premises almost never get you all the way to a conclusion by themselves. Whatever the author needed and didn't say is an assumption. This test asks about assumptions two ways, and the two are opposites, so learn them as a pair.`,
          `The first is the necessary assumption: something the argument can't live without. Here's how you find one. Take the candidate, deny it plainly, and read the argument again. If the denial wrecks it, the assumption was necessary. If the argument limps on, it wasn't. My dryer argument: the left dryer did my load in thirty eight minutes and the right one took fifty, so the owner should put the big loads on the left. Try this one. The left dryers don't slow way down when a load is big. Deny it. Now they choke on big loads, and my one load tells the owner nothing about big ones. Wrecked. Necessary.`,
          `Deny the plain way. The denial of "all" is "not all," not "none." The denial of "some" is "none." Swing to the far opposite and everything starts to look necessary.`,
          `Here's the pair that trips people. Say an argument only needs one of the left dryers to work. Candidate: some of the left dryers work. Deny it: none of them work. Wrecked, so it's necessary. Candidate: none of the left dryers are broken. Deny it: some are broken. The argument shrugs, because one working dryer was all it needed. Too strong.`,
          `And notice what the right answer looks like here. Modest. Often it's something you'd shrug at. The trap is a choice that would help a lot but goes further than the argument needs. "Every big load in the world dries faster on the left" would help me. I don't need it.`],
        terms: [[`Assumption`,`A claim an argument needs but never states.`],[`Necessary assumption`,`An unstated claim the argument depends on. If it's false, the argument falls apart.`],[`Denial test`,`Deny a candidate assumption plainly. If the argument collapses, the assumption was necessary.`]],
        exam: `Necessary assumption stems say the argument depends on, requires, or relies on an assumption. Deny each choice and keep the one whose denial sinks the argument. Choices stronger than the argument needs are the usual trap.` },
      { h: `Enough`,
        body: [`The second kind runs the other way. A sufficient assumption is one that, added to the premises, makes the conclusion follow. Not likely. Follow, with no room left. You're not asking what the argument can't live without. You're asking what would finish it.`,
          `So hunt for the new idea. Find a word in the conclusion the premises never mentioned, and build a bridge to it. Every dryer on the left was serviced in May, so the left dryers won't break down this winter. The premises talk about service. The conclusion talks about breaking down. The bridge: no dryer serviced in May will break down this winter. Add that and the conclusion has to be true.`,
          `Here strong is good. Sufficient answers are full of "all," "every," "only," and "any," because a guarantee is a strong thing. The trap runs the other direction now: a modest choice that helps without finishing.`,
          `A lot of these are conditionals. "If it was serviced in May, it won't break" says service is enough. It doesn't say service is the only way to stay running. You can run a conditional backward only if you flip both halves and deny both: if it broke, it wasn't serviced in May. That's the contrapositive, and it's always as true as the original. Flipping without denying is how most people lose these.`],
        terms: [[`Sufficient assumption`,`An unstated claim that, added to the premises, guarantees the conclusion.`],[`Conditional`,`An if and then claim. The "if" part is enough for the "then" part, not the other way round.`],[`Contrapositive`,`A conditional flipped and denied on both sides. If A then B becomes if not B then not A, and it's always as true as the original.`]],
        exam: `Sufficient assumption stems ask which choice, if assumed, allows the conclusion to be properly drawn, or which one the conclusion follows logically from. Find the idea that appears only in the conclusion, then pick the choice that connects it to the premises.` },
      { h: `Needed isn't enough`,
        body: [`Keep the two straight with a plain case. To sit for this test you need a registration. Having one doesn't put a score on your record. Showing up and answering does that. A requirement is a floor. A guarantee is a ceiling. Most real arguments need several floors, and no one of them is enough.`,
          `Plenty of bad arguments take a requirement and treat it as a guarantee, or the other way round. You'll meet that as a flaw soon. For now, every time you read a conclusion, ask both questions. What does this need? What would be enough? They have different answers more often than you'd think. Once in a while one statement is both, an exact bridge, and it'll be right on either kind of question.`,
          `Here's a drill I make everybody do. Take any conclusion and write two lists under it in pencil. On the left goes everything the argument needs: deny each one and watch it fall. On the right goes the one sentence that would finish it. The left list is usually long and dull. The right one is usually a single line, and it usually says "only" or "every." If your right hand line is modest, it's in the wrong column.`],
        terms: [[`Necessary condition`,`Something that must be true for another thing to be true. Required, but not enough on its own.`],[`Sufficient condition`,`Something that, if true, guarantees another thing is true.`]],
        exam: `When a flaw choice says the argument treats something required for a result as enough to bring it about, this is the mistake. Check whether the premises only show the thing was needed.` },
      { h: `What the page lets you say`,
        body: [`Reading Comprehension asks the same kind of question about documents. A detail question wants what the passage states. Put your finger on the line. If you can't point to it, it isn't stated, however obvious it feels. Detail traps love to borrow the passage's words and change one: the bar becomes the lot, several becomes one.`,
          `An inference question wants what the passage lets you conclude without saying it. Still close to the text. Think of it as an assumption run backward: given what's on the page, what has to be true, or nearly? Not what's likely in the world. If a report names the people interviewed and says everyone else had left before the officers came, you can infer the others weren't interviewed at the scene. You can't infer why.`,
          `A meaning in context question names a word and asks what it means where it sits. The dictionary sense is usually the trap. Read the sentence before and the sentence after, put your own word in the slot, and only then look at the choices. A word in one report may not mean what it means in the next one.`,
          `Two habits help. When a detail answer looks right, find the exact line before you pick it. When an inference answer looks right, ask whether the passage would have to be wrong for that answer to be false. If it wouldn't, you're guessing.`],
        terms: [[`Specific detail`,`A question answered by a line the passage states.`],[`Inference`,`A claim the passage supports without stating it, staying close to the text.`],[`Meaning in context`,`What a word or phrase means as the passage uses it, which may not be its usual sense.`]],
        exam: `Detail stems start "according to the passage." Inference stems say "it can be inferred," "suggests," or "most strongly supports." Meaning stems ask what a word or phrase most nearly means as used in the passage. Go back to the line every time.` }
    ],
    video: [`TFXdAfDzbw4`,`Sufficient Assumption vs. Necessary Assumption`,`LSAT Demon`,`8:23`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f2s1`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR04`, title:`The line`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Tuesday, December 1, Ana had moved one wall of banker's boxes, and behind them was a whiteboard nobody had written on in years. A phone number in one corner had faded to a blue ghost. Ana wiped it off with her sleeve.`,
        `Dani had brought the last of the turkey up the stairs in foil. Ana looked at it longer than she'd looked at the police report. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nobody brings me food,"</span> she said. She ate all of it standing at the whiteboard and didn't say thank you, and Dicta watched every bite.`,
        `Across the top, in small square capitals, she wrote the sentence from the first night: THE MAN CONSUELO BACA SAW WAS LUIS RUIZ. Under it she drew a line, a long one, all the way across.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Under the line goes everything they never said and couldn't do without,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Start with the jacket. Consuelo saw red. He wore red. What did they have to believe for red to point at him?"</span>`,
        `Dani thought about a refinery crew in matching jackets, and a city full of people who'd come for the Red Sox. On the rug Dicta sighed, like she'd heard this one before.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That he was the only man in a red jacket anywhere near that lot that whole night."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Ana writes ONLY on the board and puts a question mark after it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That would make it airtight. They didn't need airtight. Deny yours. One other guy in red walks past on Highway 2. Is their case dead?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"No. It's worse."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then it wasn't necessary. Find the one where the denial kills it."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Too strong. Deny it: there was one other man in red nearby. The State's argument gets weaker, but it doesn't die. A necessary assumption is the floor the argument stands on, and this is more than the floor.`},
        {t:`"That he'd been angry enough at Travis to go back out to the lot looking for him."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Motive,"</span> Ana says, like it's a brand of cigarette she quit. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Different argument. A jacket doesn't care how anybody felt. What does the jacket need?"</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Not needed. Angry or calm, the jacket inference runs the same. Deny it, and red points exactly where it pointed a minute ago.`},
        {t:`"That red jackets weren't so common around there that red could mean anybody."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`1. RED WASN'T COMMON, in those square capitals, under the line. Ana caps the marker. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Deny it. Say red's everywhere that night. What's their jacket worth now?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Nothing. It's a color."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Mm hm."</span> Dani starts in on the World Series, the Boston people, the crew. Up comes the pencil. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Evidence. Later. Tonight we only write what they needed."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Run the denial: red jackets all over that lot. Now a man in red could be any of a dozen guys, and the name falls off. And look how little it claims. Not the only man in red. Red wasn't everywhere, that's all.`},
        {t:`"That Travis Hensley had noticed his jacket during the shoving inside the bar."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Ana actually laughs, once, through her nose. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Travis? Travis never said boo about a jacket to anybody. Consuelo's the one who saw red."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Irrelevant. The jacket inference runs through what Consuelo saw, not what Travis saw. Deny it and nothing in the argument moves.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s2`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR04`, title:`The cuff`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana tapped the board with the eraser end. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Number two. The lab found Travis Hensley's blood on the cuff. Their closing says the blood says he did it. Did what? Where?"</span>`,
        `Dani knew this part. Her father had told the police he never touched anyone, not inside and not outside. The State had leaned on that all week at trial. A man with nothing to hide doesn't lie about a fight.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"For blood on a cuff to put him in the lot,"</span> Ana said, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"what did they have to believe about it?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That the blood didn't all get on him during the shoving inside the bar."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`2. SOME OF THE BLOOD CAME FROM THE ATTACK, NOT THE BAR. Ana steps back from it. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Here's what your father did to himself. If he'd told them, sure, we shoved, he scratched me, then the cuff is bar blood and number two is just a fight. He told them he never touched the man."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"Because he didn't."</span> Ana looks at her. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then where'd the blood come from?"</span> Dani doesn't have an answer, and Ana doesn't wait for one.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: all of the blood got there during the fight inside. Then the cuff shows a bar fight and nothing about the lot. The word "all" matters. Some blood from inside and some from outside still works for the State.`},
        {t:`"That none of Travis's blood got on his cuff, not even in the fight inside the bar."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"None is more than they needed,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Some inside and some outside still works for them. Deny yours and their case limps. It doesn't die. Make it smaller."</span> Dani does, and the smaller version goes on the board.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Too strong. Deny it: some blood got on during the fight inside. The State can still say the rest came from the lot. Watch the quantity words. The denial of "none" is "some," and "some" doesn't wreck this argument.`},
        {t:`"That he washed the jacket after he'd seen the blood on the cuff that night."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the washer's job, not the blood's,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Different line on the board. The blood argument works whether he ever looked at his sleeve or not."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Wrong argument. When he washed it, and why, belongs to the washer inference. The blood inference needs something about where the blood came from, and this says nothing about that.`},
        {t:`"That Travis was already bleeding before Consuelo ever saw him get hit."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Ana frowns at her. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Why would they need that? That'd hurt them. If he was already bleeding before he got hit, that blood could've come from anywhere."</span> Dani doesn't know why she said it. It just came out.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Backward. If this were true, it would weaken the State's argument, so it can't be something the argument depends on. A necessary assumption always helps the argument it belongs to.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s3`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC05`, title:`Two boxes`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday the boxes were on Ana's desk, two of them, ABRAMS, RUIZ 07CR on the ends in black marker. They'd come out of storage that week addressed to Ana, because Ana was his lawyer now and Dani wasn't. Phil Abrams had died in 2019. His handwriting was all over everything, slanted and fast.`,
        (G.usedthesystem
          ?`The trial index on top was the same one she'd printed at 6:40 on a Thursday night under her own login. Forty pages she already had, in a bag at home. She didn't say so. She turned past them a little too quickly.`
          :`Nothing in the first box had ever been in her basement. Her mother had kept the transcript. She hadn't had any way to keep this.`),
        `The police report was near the top, three pages stapled at the corner. Ana read it and passed it across without a word.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Commerce City Police Department, offense report, Sunday, October 28, 2007, 0215 hours">Reporting officer: D. Archuleta.<br>Dispatched 2317 hours Saturday to the rear lot of the Night Owl Tap, Highway 2, on a report of an assault. Victim Travis Hensley, 31, found on his back at the curb, unresponsive, bleeding from the head. Transported by ambulance at 2331. Hospital reports victim pronounced dead at 0140.<br>Witness Consuelo Baca, residing across the street, states that at approximately 2315 she observed from her kitchen window a male in a red jacket strike the victim with an unknown object. Subject fled on foot northbound. Witness did not see the subject's face.<br>Bartender Ronald Galvan states the victim and a regular customer, Luis Ruiz, were in a shoving fight inside the bar at approximately 2225, and that he separated them. Galvan states Ruiz left the bar at approximately 2245. Galvan states Ruiz wore a red Platte Valley work jacket, and that several men from the refinery wore the same jacket in the bar during the game.<br>Victim's sister, Shannon Hensley, by phone, states the victim called her at 2304 and said, "The guy from the bar is still out here. Come get me."<br>Remaining patrons had left before officers arrived. Names not obtained.<br>Subject not located. Referred to Investigations, Detective G. Vigil.</prop>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Start with the clock,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What does this say about when your father left? Says. Not means."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The bartender says Luis walked out of the bar at about a quarter to eleven.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The bar,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not the lot. Not the neighborhood. Galvan watched him go out a door. Anything after that door, this report doesn't know."</span> Dani underlines the word bar so hard the pencil goes through.`,
          why:`Specific detail. Put your finger on the line: Galvan states Ruiz left the bar at approximately 2245. That's what it says, at that size. Where he went after the door isn't stated anywhere on the page.`},
        {t:`Luis got in his truck and drove out of the lot at about a quarter to eleven.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Show me the lot,"</span> Ana says. Dani looks. It says bar. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Your father says he drove off at a quarter to eleven. This doesn't. Don't let him write the report for them."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. Close, with the facts stretched. The report says he left the bar. It says nothing about the lot or a truck. Detail traps borrow the passage's words and change a few.`},
        {t:`Luis told the officer himself that he'd gone home by a quarter to eleven.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`Dani looks for her father's name next to a statement. There isn't one. Nobody talked to him that night. The 10:45 is the bartender's, and it's about a door.`,
          why:`Specific detail. Not in the passage. Luis isn't quoted or interviewed anywhere in this report. You may know what he told detectives later. The question is what this page says.`},
        {t:`Luis was still out in the back lot at four minutes after eleven that night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's the State's sentence, not the report's,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The report says Travis said a guy was out there. It doesn't say who. Somebody had to put your father's name in that gap."</span>`,
          why:`Specific detail. That's an inference the prosecution drew, not a statement the report makes. The 11:04 line quotes Travis about "the guy from the bar." It never names anyone.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s4`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC05`, title:`Several men`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana let her sit with it. Downstairs the dryers had started on the evening loads, a low roll through the floor.`,
        `Then: <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Now the jacket. Same rule. What does the report say about it?"</span> Dani read the Galvan paragraph again, slower. She'd read a hundred police reports at work. She'd never read one about her own kitchen.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Galvan says Luis wore a red Platte Valley jacket, as did several refinery men.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Several,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"On the first night of the case, in the first report anybody wrote. Phil had this."</span> She doesn't say anything else about Phil. Dani reads the word again. Several. It was there before anyone had a name.`,
          why:`Specific detail. The line says Ruiz wore a red Platte Valley work jacket and that several men from the refinery wore the same jacket in the bar. A full answer gives both halves, because the second half is the one that matters.`},
        {t:`Galvan says Luis wore a red jacket just like the one Consuelo saw in the lot.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Like the one,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Show me where it says like."</span> It doesn't. Consuelo says a red jacket. Galvan says a red Platte Valley jacket, and several men in the same one. Dani had joined them herself, exactly where the State always did.`,
          why:`Specific detail. Close, but the report never compares the two jackets. Consuelo says only "a red jacket." Tying it to the one at the bar is a step the reader takes, not a line on the page.`},
        {t:`Consuelo told the officer the jacket said Platte Valley, in letters across the back.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Dani checks the Baca paragraph. A male in a red jacket. Nothing written on it. The words Platte Valley are in Galvan's paragraph, about the bar.`,
          why:`Specific detail. Wrong speaker. Consuelo's statement says only "a red jacket." The company name comes from the bartender. Detail traps move a fact from one person's mouth to another's.`},
        {t:`The officer saw Travis Hensley's blood on the cuff of the red jacket Luis wore.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`There's no cuff in the report. There's no Luis in the report after the bar, either. The blood came later, from a lab, after the jacket had been through the washer. Dani knows that from the trial. It isn't on this page.`,
          why:`Specific detail. Not in the passage. The report never mentions blood on any jacket. The lab result is real, but it comes from another document. Answer from this one.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s5`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR02`, title:`11:04`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana read the sister's line out loud, flat. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"'The guy from the bar is still out here. Come get me.'"</span> She put the page down. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"At trial they put in her phone records and put Shannon on the stand to say those words. What job was that line doing for them?"</span>`,
        `Dani had seen Shannon Hensley once, in 2008, in the hallway outside the courtroom. Shannon had looked right at her. Dani had been fifteen and had looked at the floor.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`It's a premise that goes straight to the conclusion that Luis was the one who hit Travis.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Dani draws the arrow straight from the call to her father. Ana erases the middle of it with her thumb. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It doesn't say who hit anybody. It says somebody was still out there. What's that for?"</span>`,
          why:`Role of a statement. Right kind of claim, wrong arrow. The call is a premise, but it doesn't reach the identification directly. It supports a middle step first.`},
        {t:`It backs a middle step: that the man from the fight was still there after a quarter to eleven.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana nods. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Galvan sends him out the door at 10:45. Their case needs him back in the lot at 11:15. This call sits in the middle and holds the two together."</span> She draws it on the board, a small box between two bigger ones. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Take it out and there's half an hour with nobody in it."</span>`,
          why:`Role of a statement. The call supports an intermediate claim, that the man from the bar fight was still near the lot after he left the bar, and that claim supports the identification. Arrows in and out of a middle box: that's the job.`},
        {t:`It's the main conclusion, and everything else in the case is there to support that call.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nobody had to prove Travis made a phone call,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"The phone company proved that. It's something they used, not something they were selling."</span>`,
          why:`Role of a statement. The main conclusion is the identification. The call is given as fact and used to get there, which makes it support, not the end point.`},
        {t:`It's background, there to show the jury that Travis was frightened before he was attacked.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nobody proves background twice,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And read it again. Come get me. That's a man who wants a ride."</span> Dani reads it again. It is.`,
          why:`Role of a statement. Not background, because the State built on it. And the line itself doesn't show fear. A role answer has to name the job the claim actually does, in this argument.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s6`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR04`, title:`The guy from the bar`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"So for that middle box,"</span> Ana said, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"what did they have to believe about four words? The guy from the bar."</span>`,
        `Dani tried to picture the Night Owl at 10:30 on a World Series night. Couldn't. Never been inside. At 10:30 that night she'd been fifteen, on somebody's floor in Thornton, with birthday cake and a borrowed sleeping bag that smelled like a dog.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That Shannon called the police the minute she got off the phone with her brother."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She didn't. And so what if she had,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What she did after doesn't touch who he meant."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Irrelevant. Shannon's response doesn't touch what the words referred to. Deny it and the State's reading of the call is exactly as strong as before.`},
        {t:`"That Luis was the only man in the whole bar Travis had words with that night."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Only. Again,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Say he mouthed off to two guys and shoved one. 'The guy' can still be the one he shoved. They need less than that."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Too strong. Deny it: Travis had words with somebody else too. The State still argues he meant the man he fought. That's what the argument needs, not "only."`},
        {t:`"That when Travis said 'the guy from the bar' he meant the man he'd fought."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana writes it, quote marks and all. 3. "THE GUY FROM THE BAR" WAS LUIS. Dani stares at it. <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"It was a bar full of guys."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Put that in your pocket,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You'll want it later."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it, say Travis meant somebody else from the bar, and the call says nothing about Luis. The middle box is empty. The phrase only helps the State if it points at the man from the fight.`},
        {t:`"That Travis hadn't had any more to drink than the people around him that night."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Drunk, sober, who knows,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He still said 'the guy.' What'd they need that to mean?"</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Irrelevant. Maybe his drinking matters to how far you'd trust him. The State's argument doesn't lean on it. Deny it and the words still say what they say.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s7`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC06`, title:`Names not obtained`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Saturday, December 5, the first of Ana's every other Saturdays. Ana had sorted Phil's loose notes into a pile on the corner of the desk, under a stapler, and Dani read them while Ana was on the phone with a court clerk about something else.`,
        `<prop type="doc" title="Phil Abrams's notes, loose in the front of box one">3/12/08. Galvan at prelim. Says client swung first. Get the bar tab. Who else was there?<br>4/2/08. Client won't discuss the fight. Says nothing happened.<br>5/14/08. Jail visit. Client says no stops. Ask again re daughter.<br>5/20/08. Eyewitness expert? No funds. Ask again.<br>6/3/08. Daughter tomorrow. Short cross. Be kind.</prop>`,
        `Re daughter. Her, then, twice on one page. The first was lawyer shorthand she couldn't read. The second one she could.`,
        `Be kind. Dani read that one twice. She remembered his voice, asking if she was okay to keep going, and she remembered thinking at fifteen that he was on the wrong side to be asking.`,
        `Ana hung up. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Back to the report. Who did they talk to that night? And what does that tell you about who they didn't?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The officers didn't question a single one of the bar's customers at the scene.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Remaining patrons had left. Names not obtained,"</span> Ana reads. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"A bar full of men on a World Series night, and the first report has one of them."</span> Dani looks at the note on the stapler pile. Get the bar tab. Who else was there? Phil had asked. There's nothing in the box that says anybody answered.`,
          why:`Inference. The report lists the bartender, the neighbor and the sister by phone. Travis was unresponsive, Galvan has Luis gone at 10:45, and the remaining patrons had left with names not obtained. So no customer was questioned at the scene. The report doesn't say so in those words, but it can't mean anything else.`},
        {t:`The officers didn't question the other customers because they figured they knew nothing.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Did they decide that?"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It says the patrons had left. Maybe the officers didn't care. Maybe they couldn't find them. The page tells you who wasn't asked. It doesn't tell you why."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. Half right. It's supported that the other customers weren't questioned. The reason given here isn't. An inference stays close to the text, and the text gives a fact, not a motive.`},
        {t:`Galvan was the only person who saw the two men shoving each other in the bar.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"He's the only one they wrote down,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's a different thing. Everybody in that bar saw it, probably. Nobody asked."</span>`,
          why:`Inference. Too strong, and a confusion of who was asked with who saw. The report records only Galvan's account of the fight. That says nothing about how many other people watched it.`},
        {t:`Luis was questioned at the scene by the officers before he went home that night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Dani looks for it and can't find it. Her father isn't in the report except in Galvan's mouth. He'd been gone before anybody came.`,
          why:`Inference. Not supported. The report never says officers spoke with Luis, and Galvan's statement puts him out the door at 10:45, before officers arrived. Nothing on the page backs this.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s8`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, task:`RC07`, title:`Subject`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The last line of the report was short. Subject not located. Referred to Investigations, Detective G. Vigil.`,
        `Dani had read Gene Vigil's later reports in the trial exhibits. In those, a few days on, "the subject" was her father, every time, with his date of birth after it.`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"What does subject mean,"</span> Ana said, <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"right there, in that sentence, at two in the morning?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Any suspect in the case, which by Sunday afternoon was her father.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's what it came to mean,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not what it means here. At two in the morning it's a man running north in a red jacket. Nobody had a name for him yet."</span>`,
          why:`Meaning in context. It's a suspect, so this is partly right. But in this sentence it refers to one specific unknown man, the one the report has already called "subject" twice. The later meaning doesn't belong here.`},
        {t:`The topic of the report itself, the assault in the Night Owl's back lot.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You can't locate a topic,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Read the sentence with your word in it. Topic not located. Does that mean anything?"</span> It doesn't.`,
          why:`Meaning in context. That's the dictionary sense, and it doesn't fit the sentence. "Subject not located" only makes sense if the subject is a person someone was looking for.`},
        {t:`The man in the red jacket Consuelo saw, whoever he turned out to be.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Whoever he was,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Subject fled on foot. Subject not located. Same man, the one in Consuelo's paragraph. Somewhere between this page and the next one, they wrote a name into that word."</span> Dani reads the line again. On foot.`,
          why:`Meaning in context. The report uses "subject" first right after Consuelo's account, for the man who fled on foot, and the last line uses it the same way. In this document it means that unknown man.`},
        {t:`Travis Hensley, the man the whole report was written about that night.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Travis is the victim,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They knew right where he was. The report says so in the second line."</span>`,
          why:`Meaning in context. The report calls Travis "victim" every time, and it says where he was taken. The subject is the person they couldn't find.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s9`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR04`, title:`Half an hour`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Last one,"</span> Ana said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Galvan has him out the door at 10:45. Consuelo has a man in red at 11:15. That's half an hour. What did they need to believe to cover it?"</span>`,
        `Dani looked at the board. Three lines under the long one now. She thought about her father's truck, the old Ford with the bench seat, and how she'd never once in her life seen him walk anywhere he could drive.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That Galvan was wrong, and he really left the bar later than 10:45."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They didn't need Galvan wrong,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Galvan was their witness. He could leave at 10:45 and stand in the lot till midnight. Their story never fought the bartender."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Not needed. Leaving the bar at 10:45 is perfectly consistent with being near the lot at 11:15. Deny it, Galvan was right, and the State's argument is untouched.`},
        {t:`"That he drove home first and then came back to the lot to find Travis."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's one way it could have gone,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Is it the only way? If he never left, does their case die?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"No."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Then it isn't necessary."</span>`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. One possible story, not a requirement. Deny it: he never went home in between. The State's case is fine. An assumption that's only one of several routes to the conclusion isn't one the argument depends on.`},
        {t:`"That his truck was still sitting in that lot at a quarter past eleven."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Too much,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"His truck could be a block away and their story still works. What do they need about him, not the truck?"</span> Dani crosses out truck and writes him.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Too strong. Deny it: the truck was parked down the street. The State's argument survives. What it can't survive is Luis being gone from the area.`},
        {t:`"That he was somewhere near that lot when Consuelo looked out her window."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`4. NEAR THE LOT AT 11:15. Ana underlines it twice. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Nobody ever proved that. They didn't think they had to."</span> Dani reads the report again: subject fled on foot. She thinks about a man with a truck running north on foot. She doesn't say it. Ana looks at her face. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Not tonight,"</span> Ana says.`,
          why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: he wasn't anywhere near the lot at 11:15. Then he can't be the man Consuelo saw, and everything above the line falls. It's modest, too. It doesn't say how he got there, or whether he stayed or came back. Only that he was there.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s10`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR05`, title:`A purple horse`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Wednesday, December 9, seven, basement stairs. Recording first. Same lady, same speech about where the call's from, which Dani could do in her sleep and sometimes did. Then her father, not even a hello. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I got a man on a purple horse."</span>`,
        `<span class="said" data-who="Dani">"That's you."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I figured. Never been on a horse in my life."</span> Smiling. She could hear it. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Horse has a hat on."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"She wanted you in one."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"So the horse gets it."</span> He laughed. The real one, from the chest, that she hadn't heard since July. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Smart girl. What's she like, mija? Give me one thing."</span>`,
        `She gave him three. Lupe hums when she draws, one note mostly, and gets mad if you notice. She has Teo's eyebrows. Not like them. His. She'd climbed on a chair to see his picture. Nothing on the line for a while but hiss. Then, quieter: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Your brother?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"No, Pa."</span> No okay this time. He let the hiss have it.`,
        `He asked about the lawyer. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Brennan,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I remember that name. She had my appeal."</span> A pause, long enough for the hiss. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"She lost. Wrote me two pages after, by hand. Most of them don't write."</span> Then fast, a thing he'd been carrying since Sunday: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"So she shows them the jacket's no good. Then they let me out. That's how it goes?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"I'm going to get you out of there, Pa. However long it takes. I promise."`, s:0, flag:`promisedhim`, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The line hisses. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Don't promise me things, mija,"</span> he says, very gently. <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"I've had a lot of people promise me things."</span> Then, quieter: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Thank you."</span> She hears how much he wanted it, and she knows she just gave him something nobody can back.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. This answers "what would be enough" with a promise, and a promise isn't a premise. Nothing she knows makes his release follow. On this test, the choice that guarantees too much without a reason is the trap.`},
        {t:`"If the jacket's no good, the whole case is no good. That's how it goes, Pa."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Good,"</span> he says, and he believes her, and on the bus the next morning she knows it isn't true. Take the jacket away and they still have the blood, the washer, the bartender, the call. The jacket is needed. It isn't enough.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. This treats a necessary piece as a sufficient one. Knocking out the jacket is something Ana's motion needs, but the State had other evidence. Breaking one support doesn't guarantee the conclusion falls.`},
        {t:`"If the jury had heard you tell it yourself, Pa, it would've gone the other way."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Phil said not to,"</span> he says, and that's all he says. The line is quiet for a long time. She's answered a different question, about 2008, and he's let her.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. Off the point. He asked what would make the motion work now. What might have changed the trial is a separate argument, and nothing here makes it true.`},
        {t:`"If the jacket goes and nothing else can point at you without it, then yes."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Quiet. Then: <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"You sound like her already."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Dani">"I've been sitting in her office."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Luis">"Good."</span> Knight out, his move. She gives him hers. Nobody says promise.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. This names what would actually be enough. Losing the jacket alone isn't. Losing the jacket, with nothing else able to identify him on its own, is the kind of thing that would be enough. Bridge the gap all the way, and say nothing more than that.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s11`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR05`, title:`Needed, enough`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday, December 10, Ana had drawn a second column on the whiteboard, to the right of the four lines. At the top of the left one she'd written NEEDED. At the top of the right one, ENOUGH. The right one was empty.`,
        ((s.picks||{})[0]===0
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tuesday you gave me a sentence and I told you it was too strong,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Tonight too strong might be exactly what we want."</span>`
          :`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Most people hand me tonight's answer on Tuesday, and I tell them it's too strong,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You didn't. Let's see if you can find it now."</span>`),
        `<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"One sentence. If it were true, their conclusion would follow from what they had. Not probably. Follow. What is it?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`"That he was the only man in a red jacket anywhere near that lot at 11:15."`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`Ana writes it under ENOUGH. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Consuelo saw a man in red near the lot. Your father was the only man in red near the lot. Then the man was your father. Nothing left to argue."</span> She taps the column. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"They never proved it. They never even said it. They let the jury feel it."</span>`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. Add it to the premises and the conclusion is guaranteed: the man in red was the only man in red, so it was Luis. It's stronger than anything the State needed, which is fine here. Sufficient answers are allowed to be strong.`},
        {t:`"That red jackets weren't so common around there that red could mean anybody."`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's Tuesday's,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"It's what they needed. Needing it doesn't get you there. Three men in red is not common, and it's still three men."</span> She points at the empty column. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Enough."</span>`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. This was the necessary one. It's a floor the argument stands on, but even if it's true, a few men in red could still be near the lot, and the conclusion doesn't follow. Needed isn't enough.`},
        {t:`"That he had a reason to go after Travis again after their fight in the bar."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"A reason isn't a fact,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Half the men in that bar had a reason to be mad at somebody. Did it make them the man in the lot?"</span>`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. Motive might help a little, but adding it doesn't make the identification follow. Plenty of people with a reason don't act on it.`},
        {t:`"That Consuelo Baca told the officer and the jury the truth, every word."`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Ana">"She did,"</span> Ana says. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"And the truth she told was a man in a red jacket. That's the premise. It doesn't get you to a name."</span>`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. Her honesty is already built into the premise. It doesn't connect "a man in red" to "Luis," which is the gap the conclusion needs closed.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s12`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, task:`LR05`, title:`Her column`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Ana let her take the dispatch folder home. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"That's Phil's original. Not a copy."</span> Ana held it a second before she let go. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"Bring it back Tuesday. Read it at your table, not on the bus."</span> It went in her bag next to the prep book. The January test was five weeks out. She'd done six practice questions all week.`,
        `At home she didn't open the folder. She sat at the kitchen table under the photo on the fridge and drew two columns on an envelope. NEEDED. ENOUGH. Then she did what she'd never done in nineteen years. She wrote her own doubt across the top, the way you'd write the State's conclusion, so she could look at it. Maybe he did it.`,
        `NEEDED was easy. She'd been living in it. What would be ENOUGH? What one thing, if she knew it was true, would end it?`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That the State's argument doesn't hold up against him, start to finish.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`She writes it and hears Ana before she's done. Breaking an argument doesn't prove the opposite. If the State's case falls apart, she'll know they didn't prove it. She won't know he didn't do it. She crosses it out, neatly, so she can still read it.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. A broken argument means the conclusion wasn't proved. It doesn't mean the conclusion is false. To end the doubt she'd need something that makes "he didn't do it" follow, and this doesn't.`},
        {t:`That Teo really was at Abuela's that whole night, like Pa always tells it.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She writes it and stares at it. Teo at Abuela's doesn't put Pa anywhere. It's the part of the story she's never once questioned, and it doesn't hold up anything about the lot. She doesn't know why it was the first thing her hand wrote.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. Irrelevant to the conclusion. Where Teo spent the night says nothing about where Luis was at 11:15. A sufficient assumption has to connect to the claim you're trying to reach.`},
        {t:`That he was somewhere else at a quarter past eleven, anywhere but that lot.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:1,conf:0,health:4},
          after:`She writes it under ENOUGH. Somewhere else at 11:15. If that's true, he's not the man in the lot, and there's nothing left to doubt. Then she looks at it for a long time, because nobody has ever said where. He says home. Home alone. She was in Thornton. Nobody was home to see him come in.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. If Luis was somewhere else at 11:15, he couldn't be the man Consuelo saw, and "he didn't do it" follows. That's a bridge that closes the gap all the way, which is what a sufficient assumption does.`},
        {t:`That he's never lied to me about anything that really mattered. Not once.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`She writes it and knows it's the one she wants. It's also not a fact about a parking lot. People who never lie still get things wrong, and people who lie about one thing don't always lie about everything. It doesn't close anything.`,
          why:`Sufficient assumption. A claim about his character doesn't make the conclusion follow. Even if he'd never lied to her, that doesn't place him anywhere at 11:15.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f2s11`, title:`Necessary or sufficient`, intro:`Ana's index cards again. Each card has an argument and one candidate assumption. Is the assumption necessary to the argument, sufficient for it, both, or neither?`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Card one. "Marco passed the river guide exam, so Marco can lead raft trips on the Arkansas." Candidate: Anyone who passes the river guide exam can lead raft trips on the Arkansas.`, choices:[`Necessary only`,`Sufficient only`,`Both`,`Neither`], answer:1, why:`Add it and the conclusion follows, so it's sufficient. But deny it: some people who pass can't lead trips on the Arkansas. Marco can still be one of the ones who can, so the argument isn't sunk. It needs passing to count for Marco, not for everyone.`},
      {prompt:`Card two. Same argument. Candidate: The exam Marco passed is the one that applies to trips on the Arkansas.`, choices:[`Necessary only`,`Sufficient only`,`Both`,`Neither`], answer:0, why:`Deny it: he passed an exam for some other river. Then his passing says nothing about the Arkansas, and the argument collapses. So it's necessary. It isn't enough, though, because passing the right exam may still not be all it takes.`},
      {prompt:`Card three. "Ana's office is on the second floor, and the building has no elevator. So everyone who visits Ana's office climbs stairs." Candidate: Without an elevator, the stairs are the only way up to the second floor.`, choices:[`Necessary only`,`Sufficient only`,`Both`,`Neither`], answer:2, why:`Add it and every visitor must take the stairs, so it's sufficient. Deny it, say there's a ramp out back, and the argument falls, so it's necessary too. An exact bridge is both.`},
      {prompt:`Card four. Same argument. Candidate: Most of Ana's visitors are lawyers.`, choices:[`Necessary only`,`Sufficient only`,`Both`,`Neither`], answer:3, why:`Who the visitors are has nothing to do with how they get upstairs. Neither.`},
      {prompt:`Card five. "The witness was a hundred yards away, at night. So her description of the car's color is unreliable." Candidate: Nobody can reliably see a car's color from a hundred yards away at night.`, choices:[`Necessary only`,`Sufficient only`,`Both`,`Neither`], answer:1, why:`If nobody can, then she couldn't, and the conclusion follows. But the argument doesn't need "nobody." It could survive a few people with remarkable eyes, so it's sufficient, not necessary.`},
      {prompt:`Card six. Same argument. Candidate: Distance and darkness can affect how well a person sees color.`, choices:[`Necessary only`,`Sufficient only`,`Both`,`Neither`], answer:0, why:`Deny it: distance and darkness never affect color vision. Then the premises give no reason to doubt her, and the argument is gone. Necessary. But "can affect" doesn't show they did here, so it isn't sufficient.`}
    ]},
  passages: {
    p1: { title:`Statement of Shannon Hensley at sentencing, August 15, 2008`,
      text:[`Your Honor, my name is Shannon Hensley. Travis was my big brother. He was thirty one. He hung ductwork for a living and he was good at it, and he was the one in our family who remembered everybody's birthday. He had two tickets to Game 4 that Sunday. He bought them for our dad, who can't stand up long enough anymore to use them.`,
        `At 11:04 that night my phone rang. I know the time because I've looked at my call log more times than I can count. He said, "The guy from the bar is still out here. Come get me." He didn't sound scared. He sounded tired and kind of embarrassed, like he'd done something dumb and wanted a ride home. I told him twenty minutes. I was in my pajamas. I had to find my keys.`,
        `When I got there, there were police cars and an ambulance in the lot, and they wouldn't let me near him. He died before two in the morning. I never talked to him again. The last thing I ever said to my brother was "twenty minutes."`,
        `People keep telling me this sentence will give me closure. I don't know what that word means. Mr. Ruiz has a family, and I've watched them in this courtroom every day, and I'm sorry for what they're losing. I'm not sorry enough to ask you for less. I'm asking for the most the law allows, because my brother doesn't get any more years at all.`] },
    p2: { title:`Dani's practice set: the planet that wasn't there`,
      text:[`In 1846 the French astronomer Urbain Le Verrier found a planet with arithmetic. For decades Uranus, then the most distant planet known, had drifted from the path that Newton's law of gravitation predicted for it. The errors were small, but they didn't go away. Le Verrier reasoned that if the law was right, something unseen had to be pulling on Uranus, and he calculated where that something should be. He sent his figures to the Berlin Observatory, and on the first night of searching an astronomer there found Neptune within about a degree of the predicted position.`,
        `The discovery was celebrated as a triumph of the law itself, and in one sense it was. But the reasoning rested on a quieter premise. To conclude that a planet existed, Le Verrier had to assume that Newton's law was complete: that the leftover motion of Uranus could only be the work of more matter. Neptune's appearance seemed to confirm that assumption, and few astronomers asked whether it had actually been tested, or whether a planet was the only thing that could produce the same leftover motion.`,
        `In 1859 the same reasoning met a harder case. Mercury, the innermost planet, also strayed from its predicted orbit. The point of its closest approach to the Sun crept forward a little faster than Newton's law allowed, even after the pull of every known planet was counted. Le Verrier, by then director of the Paris Observatory, proposed a small planet inside Mercury's orbit, too close to the Sun to see except during an eclipse or as a dot crossing the Sun's face. It even got a name, Vulcan. Over the following decades observers reported sightings, some of them confident, and every report fell apart under checking. The search went on for about half a century.`,
        `The answer, when it came, didn't involve a planet. In 1915 Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity, which describes gravity differently, accounted for Mercury's drift almost exactly, with nothing added to the solar system. The leftover motion had never been evidence of missing matter. It was evidence that the law, so reliable everywhere else, was incomplete.`,
        `The two episodes are often told as a success and a failure, but that framing misses what they share. In both, the observations were accurate and the arithmetic was sound. What differed was whether the unstated premise happened to be true. For Uranus, a complete law plus an unseen planet was the right explanation, so the method worked. For Mercury, the same method, applied with the same care, pointed at an object that didn't exist, because the premise it rested on was the thing that had failed. Neptune's discovery didn't show that the premise was safe. It showed only that, that time, nothing had broken it.`] }
  },
  quiz: [
    {q:`Archaeologist: The pottery shards found in the collapsed storeroom at the Tolland site are coated with a lead glaze that potters in this region didn't begin using until the 1300s. So the storeroom was still in use in or after the 1300s.<br><br>The archaeologist's argument depends on assuming which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`No potter anywhere in the world used lead glazes before the 1300s.`,`The shards weren't carried into the storeroom long after it fell out of use.`,`Most of the pottery found at the Tolland site was made by local potters.`,`The storeroom collapsed at some point before the end of the 1400s.`,`Lead glazes cost more to make than the glazes they replaced in the region.`],
      a:1, why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: the shards were dropped into the ruin by later visitors, long after the storeroom was abandoned. Then the glaze dates the visitors, not the storeroom, and the conclusion has nothing under it. The worldwide claim is far stronger than an argument about this region needs. Where most of the site's pottery came from, when the storeroom collapsed, and what glazes cost don't touch the inference.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Anyone who pays the senior rate at the Clearwater pool must first show the staff an ID giving a birth date more than sixty five years ago. Hal, a retired bus driver, swims at Clearwater most weekday mornings, and on Monday he paid the senior rate there. So Hal must be over sixty five.<br><br>Which one of the following is an assumption on which the argument depends?`,
      opts:[`Hal has paid the senior rate at the Clearwater pool more than once.`,`Everyone over sixty five who uses the pool pays the senior rate there.`,`The pool's staff check proof of age more carefully than most pools do.`,`The ID that Hal showed the pool's staff gave his own real birth date.`,`Hal wouldn't have used the pool if he'd had to pay the full price.`],
      a:3, why:`Necessary assumption. The premise only says Hal showed an ID with a qualifying birth date. Deny that it was his real birth date and the argument can't reach his actual age. How often he's paid, how careful the staff are compared with other pools, and what he'd have done at full price don't matter. Everyone over sixty five paying the senior rate reverses the conditional and isn't needed.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Biologist: Bats in the Arden Forest rarely catch tiger moths, though the same bats readily catch other moths of similar size and speed. Tiger moths, unlike the others, make rapid clicking sounds whenever a bat closes in. So the clicks must be what protects tiger moths from Arden's bats.<br><br>The biologist's argument requires assuming which one of the following?`,
      opts:[`The bats in the Arden Forest are able to hear the clicks tiger moths make.`,`Clicking is the only defense tiger moths have against any of their predators.`,`Bats in other forests catch tiger moths more often than Arden's bats do.`,`Tiger moths click more rapidly than any other insect in the Arden Forest.`,`Arden's bats would starve if they couldn't catch the other kinds of moths.`],
      a:0, why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: the bats can't hear the clicks. Then the clicks can't be what protects the moths from them, and the conclusion fails. "The only defense against any predator" is far stronger than needed. Other forests, other insects, and the bats' diet don't bear on whether the clicks work here.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Since the Pinyon Dam began releasing water from the bottom of its reservoir, where the water is far colder than at the surface, the trout population in Cedar Creek below the dam has fallen by half. Trout eggs fail to hatch when water stays below a certain temperature. Wildlife officials conclude that the cold releases caused the decline.<br><br>The officials' conclusion relies on which one of the following assumptions?`,
      opts:[`No other species of fish in Cedar Creek has declined since the releases began.`,`Cold water harms every species of fish that lives in Cedar Creek below the dam.`,`The dam will keep releasing bottom water for at least another decade or more.`,`Trout in nearby creeks without dams have held steady over the same years.`,`Water reaching the spawning areas in spawning season gets cold enough to stop hatching.`],
      a:4, why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: the water never gets cold enough at the spawning areas to stop eggs from hatching. Then the mechanism the officials rely on never operates, and the causal claim loses its support. Other species declining or not, every species being harmed, and the dam's future aren't required. Steady trout elsewhere would strengthen the conclusion, but the argument doesn't depend on it.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Every member of the Ridge Runners hiking club who finished the Aspen Loop this fall received a club patch, which the club handed out at its November meeting. Priya, who often hikes in the area with friends, did not receive a club patch this fall. Therefore, Priya is not a member of the Ridge Runners.<br><br>The conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?`,
      opts:[`Only members of the Ridge Runners received club patches this fall.`,`Priya did not finish the Aspen Loop at any point this fall.`,`Most members of the Ridge Runners finished the Aspen Loop this fall.`,`Priya finished the Aspen Loop this fall.`,`Some people who aren't members finished the Aspen Loop this fall.`],
      a:3, why:`Sufficient assumption. Members who finished got a patch. If Priya finished and got no patch, she can't be a member. "Only members got patches" runs the wrong direction and tells you nothing about someone without one. If she didn't finish, the rule never applies to her. "Most members" and nonmembers finishing don't guarantee anything about Priya.`, task:`LR05`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Inspector: Any kitchen that stores raw meat on shelves above cooked food creates a risk of contamination. At the Willow Café, raw chicken is kept on the shelf directly above the cooked rice. So the Willow Café must be cited.<br><br>Which one of the following, if assumed, enables the inspector's conclusion to be properly drawn?`,
      opts:[`Raw chicken carries a greater risk of contamination than other raw meats do.`,`Every kitchen that creates a risk of contamination must be cited.`,`The Willow Café has been cited for other kinds of violations in the past.`,`Only kitchens that store raw meat above cooked food must be cited.`,`Most kitchens that store raw meat this way have been cited before now.`],
      a:1, why:`Sufficient assumption. The premises get the café to "creates a risk." The conclusion is about being cited. The bridge is a rule that every kitchen creating a risk must be cited. "Only" kitchens like this must be cited reverses the direction and doesn't guarantee this one is. Chicken's relative risk, past violations, and what happened to other kitchens don't finish the argument.`, task:`LR05`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`The rules of the Wexford Essay Prize allow a late entry only if the entrant requested an extension before the deadline. Marisol's essay arrived two days late, and she requested an extension only after the deadline had passed. So the judges should not read Marisol's essay.<br><br>Which one of the following, if assumed, allows the conclusion to be properly drawn?`,
      opts:[`Marisol's essay wouldn't have won the prize even if the judges had read it.`,`Most entrants who ask for extensions do so before the deadline has passed.`,`Judges should not read any entry that the rules of the prize don't allow.`,`The judges have read some late entries in earlier years of the prize.`,`An entrant who requests an extension in time will always be granted one.`],
      a:2, why:`Sufficient assumption. The premises show the rules don't allow Marisol's entry. The conclusion is about what the judges should read. The bridge: judges shouldn't read entries the rules don't allow. Whether she'd have won, what most entrants do, earlier years, and timely requests don't connect "not allowed" to "shouldn't be read."`, task:`LR05`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Economist: When a town loses its only grocery store, its residents' diets get worse, because the nearest fresh food is suddenly a long drive away and convenience stores fill the gap. The town of Brightwater recently lost its only grocery store when its owners retired. Therefore, diet related illness in Brightwater will increase within the next ten years.<br><br>The economist's conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is assumed?`,
      opts:[`Brightwater's residents ate healthier diets than most towns' residents before.`,`Most towns that lose a grocery store get a new one within ten years.`,`Diet related illness has increased in some towns that lost their only store.`,`Some Brightwater residents will drive to stores in other towns for food.`,`In any town where diets worsen, diet related illness rises within ten years.`],
      a:4, why:`Sufficient assumption. The premises get Brightwater's diets worse. The conclusion is about illness within ten years. The bridge connecting the two is a rule that worse diets bring more illness within ten years. "Some towns" is support, not a guarantee. A new store or driving elsewhere would cut against the conclusion, and how healthy residents were before doesn't connect the two ideas.`, task:`LR05`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`]},
    {q:`Planning board member: Some residents want the old rail depot torn down and replaced with parking, since the downtown lots fill up on weekends. But the depot should be kept. The lots fill up only on weekend afternoons, the depot could hold a weekend market that would draw shoppers downtown, and a new garage two blocks away opens next year.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion of the board member's argument?`,
      opts:[`The downtown parking lots fill up only on weekend afternoons.`,`The depot should be replaced with parking for downtown shoppers.`,`A weekend market in the depot would draw shoppers to downtown.`,`The old rail depot should be kept rather than torn down.`,`A new parking garage will open two blocks away next year.`],
      a:3, why:`Main conclusion. "But the depot should be kept" turns against the residents' view, and the three facts after it are the reasons. Replacing it with parking is the view the board member rejects. The weekend afternoons, the market, and the new garage are premises.`, task:`LR01`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Columnist: Many people believe reading on screens harms comprehension, and some studies seem to agree, finding that students recall less from screen reading. But those studies mostly tested students under time pressure, when readers of any medium skim. Given unlimited time, the gap largely disappears. So the belief that screens themselves harm comprehension is not well supported.<br><br>The claim that the studies mostly tested students under time pressure plays which one of the following roles in the columnist's argument?`,
      opts:[`It is the main conclusion that the columnist's argument draws.`,`It is a finding the columnist presents as support for the popular belief.`,`It is offered to cast doubt on evidence for a view the columnist rejects.`,`It is a general principle the argument uses to justify its conclusion.`,`It is a claim the columnist concedes before turning to the main point.`],
      a:2, why:`Role of a statement. The studies support the popular belief, and the time pressure point undercuts those studies, which serves the conclusion that the belief isn't well supported. It isn't the conclusion. It works against the belief, not for it. It's a specific fact about the studies, not a general principle. And the columnist asserts it rather than conceding it.`, task:`LR02`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`To receive a Marston Foundation grant, a community group must submit a complete application by March 1. The Eastside Tenants' Union, which has run a free legal clinic for renters for six years, submitted its complete application in January. So the Eastside Tenants' Union will receive a Marston Foundation grant.<br><br>The reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument`,
      opts:[`treats something required for a grant as if it guaranteed one`,`assumes groups that apply late are never given a grant at all`,`relies on the foundation's rules without saying who wrote them`,`takes for granted the foundation has money to give this year`,`fails to consider the union may not want the grant it applied for`],
      a:0, why:`Flaw. Filing by March 1 is necessary for a grant, not sufficient. Meeting a requirement doesn't guarantee the result. The argument doesn't rely on what happens to late applicants. Who wrote the rules doesn't matter. The money and the union's wishes aren't where the reasoning goes wrong; the step from requirement to guarantee is.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`, tags:[`COND`]},
    {q:`Gardening columnist: The fertilizer Greenrow went on sale three years ago, and in that time no study has found that it harms honeybees. Many gardeners worry about bees, and some have stopped using fertilizer altogether. Gardeners who want to protect bees can therefore use Greenrow without worry.<br><br>Which one of the following most accurately describes a flaw in the columnist's reasoning?`,
      opts:[`It assumes what is true of honeybees is true of every kind of insect.`,`It treats a lack of evidence of harm as proof that there is no harm.`,`It relies on studies paid for by the company that makes the fertilizer.`,`It concludes that a product sold for three years must be sold forever.`,`It confuses gardeners who protect bees with gardeners who keep hives.`],
      a:1, why:`Flaw. No study finding harm isn't the same as a study finding no harm. There may have been few studies, or ones that didn't look. The argument stays with honeybees, never mentions who paid for any study, says nothing about how long the product will be sold, and doesn't mix up two kinds of gardeners.`, task:`LR06`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`According to Shannon Hensley, how did her brother sound when he called her that night?`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`tired and embarrassed, like someone who wanted a ride home`,`frightened of the man who had followed him out of the bar`,`angry, and ready to go back in and finish the fight`,`calm, as though nothing unusual had happened at all`,`confused, and unable to tell her where in the lot he was`],
      a:0, why:`Specific detail. She says he didn't sound scared, and that he sounded tired and kind of embarrassed, like he'd done something dumb and wanted a ride home. Frightened contradicts her. Nothing suggests anger or confusion, and embarrassed isn't calm.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`It can be inferred from the statement that Shannon Hensley`, passage:`p1`,
      opts:[`had been at the Night Owl Tap earlier that evening with her brother`,`saw the attack on her brother from the far side of the parking lot`,`was somewhere other than the bar when her brother called her`,`believes the defendant's family should also be punished`,`has spoken with the defendant privately about her brother`],
      a:2, why:`Inference. She was in her pajamas, had to find her keys, and told him she'd be twenty minutes, so she wasn't at the bar. She arrived after the police and the ambulance, so she didn't see the attack. She says she's sorry for the defendant's family. Nothing suggests she was at the bar earlier or has spoken with him.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main point of the passage?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Le Verrier's prediction of Neptune proved that Newton's law of gravitation was complete.`,`Mercury's odd motion was finally explained when astronomers found a planet near the Sun.`,`Astronomers in the 1800s were too quick to trust confident reports of new planets.`,`General relativity replaced Newton's law because Newton's law failed to predict Neptune.`,`Sound reasoning found Neptune and failed for Vulcan because its unstated premise held only once.`],
      a:4, why:`Main idea. The passage tells both episodes to show they shared a method and a hidden premise, and that the premise held for Uranus and failed for Mercury. The last paragraph says Neptune didn't prove the premise safe, which contradicts the first choice. No planet explained Mercury. Doubtful sightings are a detail. And Newton's law did predict Neptune.`, task:`RC01`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`According to the passage, Le Verrier proposed the planet Vulcan in order to account for`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`the point of Mercury's closest approach to the Sun moving forward too fast`,`small errors in the path of Uranus that Newton's law couldn't explain`,`the failure of the Berlin astronomers to find Neptune on the first night`,`confident sightings of a small planet reported during a solar eclipse`,`the predictions about Mercury's orbit made by general relativity`],
      a:0, why:`Specific detail. The third paragraph says Mercury's closest approach to the Sun crept forward faster than Newton's law allowed, and Le Verrier proposed a planet inside its orbit. Uranus's errors led to Neptune, not Vulcan. Berlin found Neptune on the first night. The sightings came after the proposal. Relativity came decades later.`, task:`RC05`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Which one of the following can most reasonably be inferred from the passage?`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`Le Verrier began to doubt Newton's law once Neptune was found near his predicted position.`,`Observers who reported seeing Vulcan were deliberately trying to deceive other astronomers.`,`Had Newton's law been incomplete for Uranus, the same method might have misled Le Verrier.`,`General relativity predicts that a small planet lies inside the orbit of Mercury.`,`Most astronomers had stopped searching for Vulcan within a few years after 1859.`],
      a:2, why:`Inference. The method worked for Uranus only because the premise that the law was complete held there. The passage shows the same method, with a failed premise, pointed at a planet that didn't exist, so it could have done the same for Uranus. Nothing says Le Verrier doubted the law or that observers lied. Relativity explained Mercury with nothing added. The search lasted about half a century.`, task:`RC06`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`As used in the second paragraph, the word "complete" most nearly means`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`finished being written down and published for other astronomers`,`accepted by every working astronomer of the time without dispute`,`able to predict exactly where every planet would be seen on any given night`,`free of any errors in the arithmetic or the telescope measurements`,`describing gravity fully, so only more matter could explain a deviation`],
      a:4, why:`Meaning in context. The passage explains "complete" right after the colon: the leftover motion of Uranus could only be the work of more matter. A complete law, here, leaves nothing out of how gravity works, so any motion it can't explain has to come from matter nobody has counted yet. Publication and acceptance are other senses of the word. Predicting every planet's position is what the law was used for, not what "complete" means. Arithmetic errors are ruled out separately in the last paragraph.`, task:`RC07`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`The last two sentences of the passage function primarily to`, passage:`p2`,
      opts:[`explain why astronomers kept searching for Vulcan for half a century`,`deny that one success showed the method's premise could be trusted`,`suggest that Le Verrier should have doubted the Berlin observations`,`concede that the Uranus calculation contained an arithmetic error`,`predict that general relativity will itself be found incomplete`],
      a:1, why:`Function. The two sentences answer the success and failure framing: Neptune didn't show the premise was safe, only that nothing broke it that time. The long search for Vulcan is in the third paragraph, not here. The passage never doubts the Berlin observation or the arithmetic; it says the observations were accurate and the arithmetic sound. And it makes no prediction about relativity.`, task:`RC08`, domain:`Reading Comprehension`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Hospital administrator: Last year our emergency department began texting patients a reminder to fill their prescriptions the day after they were discharged. Of the patients discharged in the twelve months before the program, 31 percent came back to the emergency department within thirty days; of those discharged in the twelve months since, only 24 percent did. Some board members credit a new urgent care clinic that opened nearby last spring. But the clinic treats mostly minor injuries, and most of our thirty day returns are for complications of the condition that brought the patient in. So the reminder program is probably responsible for most of the drop.<br><br>Which one of the following is an assumption on which the administrator's argument depends?`,
      opts:[`Every patient who fills a prescription on time avoids the complications that would bring them back.`,`The new urgent care clinic has drawn few patients away from the hospital's emergency department.`,`Most of the patients who got the reminder texts read them on the same day they were sent.`,`Patients discharged since the program began weren't, as a group, much healthier than earlier ones.`,`The hospital's 24 percent return rate is lower than the rate at any other hospital nearby.`],
      a:3, why:`Necessary assumption. Deny it: if the patients discharged since the program were a much healthier group, fewer of them would come back with complications whether or not anyone texted them, and the drop no longer points at the reminders. The administrator rules out the clinic but never rules out a change in who was discharged. Every patient avoiding complications is far more than the argument needs. The clinic's draw doesn't matter, since it treats minor injuries and the returns are complications. When the texts were read, and how other hospitals compare, are both beside the point.`, task:`LR04`, domain:`Logical Reasoning`, level:`AN`, tags:[`CAUSE`,`STAT`]}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `She didn't open the folder until after eleven. Phil Abrams had put a tab on it that said 911 in his slanted capitals, and inside were a few pages of gray printout, faint, the kind that came off a printer nobody had changed the ribbon on.`,
    ((s.flags.promisedhim||G.promisedhim)
      ?`On the counter her phone showed the call from Wednesday, nineteen minutes. She'd told him she'd get him out. She'd said it like a move already on the board.`
      :`On the counter her phone showed the call from Wednesday, nineteen minutes. He'd asked one thing about the case and eleven about Lupe. She'd counted.`),
    (s.score>=27
      ?`Ana had said one thing as she'd walked her to the stairs. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You write down the gap before you argue with it. Keep doing that. It's the whole job."</span>`
      :`Ana had said one thing as she'd walked her to the stairs. <span class="said" data-who="Ana">"You keep arguing before you've written down the gap. Write it down first. Then argue."</span>`),
    `Most of the printout was codes. Units assigned, units on the way, a time stamp every line. On the second page somebody, Phil probably, had run a yellow highlighter across one entry, and the yellow stopped partway, right at the end of the words everyone in the case already knew. MALE RED JKT. The rest of the line was there. It had always been there. Nobody had colored it in.`,
    `She read the whole line.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Dispatch log, Saturday, October 27, 2007, page 2">2317:38 911 CALL RCVD. RP C BACA, RES ACROSS FROM REAR LOT, NIGHT OWL TAP, HWY 2.<br>2317:52 RP ADV MALE RED JKT BALL CAP STRUCK VICT W UNK OBJ, SUBJ FLED ON FOOT.<br>2318:20 VICT DOWN AT CURB, NOT MOVING. MEDICAL REQUESTED.<br>2318:41 UNIT 214 EN ROUTE.</prop>`,
    `Ball cap.`,
    `She read it again, because that was the rule. Go back and find the line. Don't answer from memory.`,
    `The envelope was still by her elbow. MAYBE HE DID IT, in her own capitals. A cap didn't put him anywhere. She knew exactly what Ana would say. She didn't cross anything out.`,
    `Then she looked up at the fridge. Her fourteenth birthday. Her father in the red jacket, laughing, and on the table in front of him the paper hat her mother had tried to put on him and he'd taken right back off.`,
    `Every picture she owned. Every Wednesday for nineteen years, when she closed her eyes on the basement stairs and saw him. Bareheaded. Not at the refinery, not at Coors Field, not at the lake in August with the sun on him. Hats make me look like my brother, he used to say, and her mother laughed every single time.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch02.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Notebook`
});
